 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Convert any mixed numbers to improper fractions",
   "For + or −: find the LCD and convert both fractions. For × or ÷: no LCD needed",
   "For ÷: keep, flip, multiply",
   "Calculate, then simplify by dividing numerator and denominator by their HCF"
  ],
  "title": "How to Add, Subtract, Multiply and Divide Fractions",
  "content": "<p>The key to fraction arithmetic is knowing when you need a <strong>common denominator</strong> and when you don't.</p><p><strong>Adding and subtracting:</strong> You must have the same denominator. Find the lowest common denominator (LCD), convert both fractions, then add or subtract the numerators only. Simplify the result if possible.</p><p><strong>Multiplying:</strong> No common denominator needed. Multiply numerators together and denominators together: \\(\\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{a \\times c}{b \\times d}\\). Cross-cancel before multiplying to keep numbers small.</p><p><strong>Dividing:</strong> Keep the first fraction, flip the second (find its reciprocal), then multiply: \\(\\frac{a}{b} \\div \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{d}{c}\\).</p><p><strong>Mixed numbers:</strong> Always convert to improper fractions before calculating. To convert: multiply the whole number by the denominator, add the numerator, keep the same denominator. Convert back at the end if the question asks for a mixed number.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Calculate</strong> \\(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{4}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> No mixed numbers, so move to step 2.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> LCD of 3 and 4 is 12. Convert: \\(\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{8}{12}\\) and \\(\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add the numerators: \\(\\frac{8}{12} + \\frac{9}{12} = \\frac{17}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Convert to mixed number: \\(\\frac{17}{12} = 1\\frac{5}{12}\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(1\\frac{5}{12}\\)</p>"
 },
